# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for ehukai.
#
# oneTBB's work-stealing scheduler (partitioner, blocked_range, start_for,
# small-object pool) coordinates worker threads with atomics and fences that
# ThreadSanitizer cannot see through, because the system libtbb shipped by
# Ubuntu's libtbb-dev is built WITHOUT -fsanitize=thread instrumentation. TSan
# therefore reports benign data races deep inside the TBB runtime whenever a
# parallel_for splits work across threads (e.g. test_Propagation's spectral
# sweeps). These are false positives, not bugs in ehukai.
#
# Real ehukai concurrency findings are NOT silenced by this file: they
# fail their own functional assertions in test_FftwWrapper (the concurrent
# shared-plan execute test) independently of TSan's race reports.
#
# To see the raw TBB reports, run with TSAN_OPTIONS unset (no suppressions).

# Races attributed to the inlined TBB scheduler templates (partitioner,
# blocked_range, start_for, small-object pool) compiled into the test binaries.
# This single pattern covers the symbolized TBB-runtime reports.
race:tbb::detail::

# When the system libtbb ships without symbols, those same runtime frames
# resolve to <null> instead of tbb::detail::, so the pattern above misses them.
# Match the uninstrumented shared library directly. Real ehukai races still
# fail their own functional assertions in test_FftwWrapper, independent of TSan.
called_from_lib:libtbb.so.12
